A||||37||billfredmike24||Anitfreeze||||5||
Z||000000||billfredmike24||12-09-1999||08:54 PM||||I'm putting my newly bought 85 gt away for the winter and was wondering if anyone knows of red antifreeze.  That's what's in my car and is what the last owner had in it.  I'm in Ontario and was wondering if it would be safe to have my car in the garage with this type of antifreeze in it, considering the freezing temps. we get in Ontario.  Thanks for you help.||||reg||5
Z||000001||Silver Bullet||12-09-1999||09:28 PM||||The red anti freeze is called dexcool.  It is much better than regular anti-freeze.  I work at Jiffy Lube, and the lowest dealer reccomendation for having to change dexcool was at 150,000 miles.  You are more then safe  to leave it there.  One thing you may want to check into, I think there is somesort of process the car has to go through, flushing i think, before you switch from regular to dex. You may want to check into that.  I hope I helped ya!!||||reg||14
Z||000002||billfredmike24||12-09-1999||11:26 PM||||thanks Silver Bullet  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000003||2FAST4U||12-10-1999||12:08 AM||||Just a little more info about Havoline Dexcool.<BR>One of the reasons Dexcool is so good is that it contains no silicates as all regular antifreeze does. Silicates are abrasive and tend to wear out water pump bearings and seals faster. I suggest everybody use Dexcool.||||reg||1
Z||000004||dogtired||12-10-1999||10:50 AM||||Just another quickie here!<BR>I purchased a 1997 Blazer (NEW) and it has the red stuff in it. The dealer said it was good for 5 years. <BR>Would a dealer LIE?    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/redface.gif">  lets not go there!<BR>Anyway I thought the 5 year thing was worth sharing  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||6
Z||000005||Monkeyman||12-10-1999||01:10 PM||||Let's see...That would make your avg mileage about 30,000/year.  I know that I don't drive that much!  But your dealer wouldn't lie to you to make more $$.  That would be unethical <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000006||fierospeeder||12-10-1999||01:17 PM||||They show that red coolant on tv, forgot whose selling it, i think its in a black container.  They talk about how it lasts way longer then the conventional antifreeze.||||reg||1
Z||000007||olympic||12-10-1999||03:07 PM||||All new cars are required by law to go 100,000 miles before it's first scheduled tune-up. Thats where we got 100,000 mile platinum plugs and 5 year anti-freeze from.||||reg||1
Z||000008||2FAST4U||12-11-1999||03:59 AM||||The red antifreeze you guys are talking about is Texaco Havoline Dexcool. You can get at most parts stores or Texaco stations. According to the jug it comes in it's guaranteed for 150,000 mi.||||reg||1
Z||000009||2birds||12-11-1999||10:28 PM||||Prestone's got a version too, says on the jug it meets the same specs.  Just don't mix and match with the green stuff.  I shifted mine over last spring when I did a complete system flush.  Make sure you pull the thermostat out while flushing, or you won't get it all.||||reg||3
Z||000010||Cooter||12-11-1999||10:49 PM||||I remember reading that when an engine is converted from green to red anti freeze, that the benefits from the red anti freeze are not as good as it would have been if the engine had the red anti freeze to begin with. Something about the way the red coats the surfaces of the system that is prohibited by the residue left behind by the green antifreeze and the article warned not to convert from to the other for risk of damage to engine seals, and specifically mentioned head gasket failures.||||reg||1
Z||000011||Monkeyman||12-12-1999||02:49 AM||||why couldn't you just flush the system really well when changing over?  What's it supposed to do to the seals and stuff?||||reg||1
Z||000012||ka4nkf||12-12-1999||09:30 PM||||I look at it this way. when you have something that does not give any problems! then why change. I been using the green stuff for years and never had any problems. besides prestone knows more about anti freeze than texaco.||||reg||1
Z||000013||2FAST4U||12-13-1999||02:52 AM||||Maby that's why Prestone makes the red stuff now too. It just took them a while to copy Texaco||||reg||1
Z||000014||theogre||12-14-1999||07:31 PM||||Actually Ethylene Glycol and Propalene Glycol never break down. It's the "additive package" that's the problem.<P>the machine that recycles antifreeze is really simple.... it's a set of filters and a centrifical speparator. Basicaly it's the same way you get cream out of the milk. Then you add a new "package" to it and it's good to go.||||reg||1
